[{"id":"physics-relativity-problem-set-q1","subject":"Physics","area":"Science","year":12,"examYear":null,"units":"4","number":1,"source":"WACE Physics Unit 4 Special Relativity Multi-Step Problem Booklet","sourceKind":"Problem booklet","sourceFile":"WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourcePath":"assets/physics/special-relativity/WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceSHA256":"9db2fd3d7d4ae4c933220198db66a9659f00752d9e0ae512f61d05d5b91aa564","sourceKeyFile":"WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceKeyPath":"assets/physics/special-relativity/WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceKeySHA256":"9db2fd3d7d4ae4c933220198db66a9659f00752d9e0ae512f61d05d5b91aa564","sourceKeyLocation":"Embedded Answers section, Questions 1–25","section":"Problem solving","sourceSection":"Problem solving","types":["Calculations"],"responseTypes":["Calculation"],"topics":["Revolutions in modern physics"],"subtopics":["Special relativity","Time dilation","Length contraction"],"mixed":false,"marks":10,"marksOrigin":"supplied","partMarks":[{"label":"a","marks":2},{"label":"b","marks":2},{"label":"c","marks":3},{"label":"d","marks":3}],"fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"text":"Atmospheric Muon DecayMuons are elementary particles created in the upper atmosphere when primary cosmic rays collide with atmospheric nuclei. A specific muon is created at an altitude of 10.0 km above sea level and travels directly towards the surface of Earth at a constant speed of v=0.995c. The rest lifetime (proper lifetime) of a muon is τ_0=2.20 μs (2.20×10^(−6) s). A detector situated on Earth's surface records the arrival of these particles. Take the speed of light in a vacuum to be c=3.00×10^(8) m s^(−1).(a) Calculate the Lorentz factor, γ, for a muon travelling at 0.995c. [2 marks] (b) Using classical (Newtonian) physics, calculate the distance the muon would travel in one rest lifetime. Hence, explain why classical mechanics predicts that virtually zero muons should reach the ground detector. [2 marks] (c) Calculate the lifetime of the muon from the reference frame of an Earth-based observer, and determine the mean distance the muon travels in this frame before decaying. [3 marks] (d) In the reference frame of the muon, calculate the contracted distance from its creation point to Earth's surface. Explain how this allows the muon to reach the ground detector from its own perspective. [3 marks]","figures":1,"bankFile":"practice-studio-physics-relativity-problem-set.json","markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","addedAt":"2026-11-07T11:13:41.482492Z","hash":"228de18c00b9c23d7da1da92cb3535ae935ddaff2dbd9de18457ce82bbf61c78"},{"id":"physics-relativity-problem-set-q2","subject":"Physics","area":"Science","year":12,"examYear":null,"units":"4","number":2,"source":"WACE Physics Unit 4 Special Relativity Multi-Step Problem Booklet","sourceKind":"Problem booklet","sourceFile":"WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourcePath":"assets/physics/special-relativity/WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceSHA256":"9db2fd3d7d4ae4c933220198db66a9659f00752d9e0ae512f61d05d5b91aa564","sourceKeyFile":"WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceKeyPath":"assets/physics/special-relativity/WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceKeySHA256":"9db2fd3d7d4ae4c933220198db66a9659f00752d9e0ae512f61d05d5b91aa564","sourceKeyLocation":"Embedded Answers section, Questions 1–25","section":"Problem solving","sourceSection":"Problem solving","types":["Calculations"],"responseTypes":["Calculation"],"topics":["Revolutions in modern physics"],"subtopics":["Special relativity","Time dilation","Length contraction"],"mixed":false,"marks":9,"marksOrigin":"supplied","partMarks":[{"label":"a","marks":2},{"label":"b","marks":2},{"label":"c","marks":3},{"label":"d","marks":2}],"fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"text":"Relativistic Interstellar VoyageAn uncrewed deep-space probe travels at a constant velocity of v=0.850c from an orbital depot near Earth to an automated research station orbiting Proxima Centauri. The distance between Earth and Proxima Centauri is measured by mission control on Earth to be L_0=4.24 light-years (4.01×10^(16) m). Both orbital stations remain at rest relative to one another.(a) Identify which observer measures the proper length of the journey and which observer measures the proper time. Justify your answer using relativistic definitions. [2 marks] (b) Calculate the total travel time in years as recorded by observers stationed on Earth. [2 marks] (c) Determine the Lorentz factor, γ, for the probe, and calculate the duration of the journey in years as registered by an onboard atomic clock. [3 marks] (d) Calculate the distance between Earth and Proxima Centauri as determined by sensors aboard the moving probe. Show that this distance is consistent with your answer to part (c). [2 marks]","figures":1,"bankFile":"practice-studio-physics-relativity-problem-set.json","markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","addedAt":"2026-11-07T11:13:41.482492Z","hash":"8b68eca9cd5056513b4d266f1aa739c18132a3cd8f712c0b975e88e9a2b2cd10"},{"id":"physics-relativity-problem-set-q3","subject":"Physics","area":"Science","year":12,"examYear":null,"units":"4","number":3,"source":"WACE Physics Unit 4 Special Relativity Multi-Step Problem Booklet","sourceKind":"Problem booklet","sourceFile":"WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourcePath":"assets/physics/special-relativity/WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceSHA256":"9db2fd3d7d4ae4c933220198db66a9659f00752d9e0ae512f61d05d5b91aa564","sourceKeyFile":"WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceKeyPath":"assets/physics/special-relativity/WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceKeySHA256":"9db2fd3d7d4ae4c933220198db66a9659f00752d9e0ae512f61d05d5b91aa564","sourceKeyLocation":"Embedded Answers section, Questions 1–25","section":"Problem solving","sourceSection":"Problem solving","types":["Calculations"],"responseTypes":["Calculation"],"topics":["Revolutions in modern physics"],"subtopics":["Special relativity","Relativistic energy","Relativistic momentum"],"mixed":false,"marks":8,"marksOrigin":"supplied","partMarks":[{"label":"a","marks":3},{"label":"b","marks":3},{"label":"c","marks":2}],"fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"text":"Relativistic Dynamics in a Particle AcceleratorIn a synchrotron facility, a proton of rest mass m_0=1.673×10^(−27) kg and rest energy E_0=938 MeV is accelerated through a series of radiofrequency cavities. After acceleration, the proton reaches an energy state where its total relativistic energy is equal to exactly 3.20 times its rest energy (E_total=3.20E_0). Take c=3.00×10^(8) m s^(−1) and 1 eV=1.602×10^(−19) J.(a) State the numerical value of the Lorentz factor, γ, and calculate the speed of the proton as a fraction of c. [3 marks] (b) Calculate the relativistic momentum of the proton at this speed in kg m s^(−1). [3 marks] (c) Calculate the work done on the proton during acceleration, expressing your answer in gigaelectronvolts (GeV). [2 marks]","figures":1,"bankFile":"practice-studio-physics-relativity-problem-set.json","markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","addedAt":"2026-11-07T11:13:41.482492Z","hash":"a03141ce08b3698c03767fe57d3b6a609ec4bd459e024a9bd071e79b2533edc7"},{"id":"physics-relativity-problem-set-q4","subject":"Physics","area":"Science","year":12,"examYear":null,"units":"4","number":4,"source":"WACE Physics Unit 4 Special Relativity Multi-Step Problem Booklet","sourceKind":"Problem booklet","sourceFile":"WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourcePath":"assets/physics/special-relativity/WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceSHA256":"9db2fd3d7d4ae4c933220198db66a9659f00752d9e0ae512f61d05d5b91aa564","sourceKeyFile":"WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceKeyPath":"assets/physics/special-relativity/WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourceKeySHA256":"9db2fd3d7d4ae4c933220198db66a9659f00752d9e0ae512f61d05d5b91aa564","sourceKeyLocation":"Embedded Answers section, Questions 1–25","section":"Problem solving","sourceSection":"Problem solving","types":["Calculations"],"responseTypes":["Calculation"],"topics":["Revolutions in modern physics"],"subtopics":["Special relativity","Time dilation"],"mixed":false,"marks":6,"marksOrigin":"supplied","partMarks":[{"label":"a","marks":3},{"label":"b","marks":3}],"fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"text":"Moving atomic clockA spacecraft moves at 0.600c relative to Earth. An onboard atomic clock measures 12.0 minutes between two signals emitted at the same point on the spacecraft. (a) Find the Lorentz factor and the Earth-frame interval. [3 marks] (b) Find the distance travelled in the Earth frame during this interval, using c=3.00×10^(8) m s⁻¹. [3 marks]","figures":1,"bankFile":"practice-studio-physics-relativity-problem-set.json","markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","addedAt":"2026-11-07T11:13:41.482492Z","hash":"72f4722bbad3efd9993050513fd6c8a6a1ec1ba17a7a52781f8bdec777ddafb4"},{"id":"physics-relativity-problem-set-q5","subject":"Physics","area":"Science","year":12,"examYear":null,"units":"4","number":5,"source":"WACE Physics Unit 4 Special Relativity Multi-Step Problem Booklet","sourceKind":"Problem booklet","sourceFile":"WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Special-Relativity-Multi-Step-Problem-Booklet.docx","sourcePath":"assets/physics/special-relativity/WACE-Physics-Unit-4-Specia